  critique: The render reads clearly as an FPGA NTT accelerator — TOP shows eight blue BRAM banks, copper DSP multiplier cylinders, the reduction spine and the right-edge twiddle/addr/controller cluster, and SIDE confirms the exploded butterfly cluster floats correctly on risers above the array. But the headline 8-parallel-BU configuration is still only half-built: the render shows just four butterfly rows, costing structural-decomposition and proportional points the prior pass flagged twice. The two reduction members are still indistinguishable opaque boxes in TOP, and there is no visible operand-return routing from the registers back to the crossbar, so the dataflow loop reads as open-ended.
  + Doubled the butterfly array from 4 to 8 parallel clusters (bu_mult/add/sub/reg 0..7) aligned 1:1 with the eight BRAM banks at z=-3.85..3.85, matching the paper's 8-BU benchmark configuration
  + Replaced the four per-cluster baseplates with one shared open butterfly-array baseplate (bu_array_base) so the eight exposed datapaths read as a single SIMD lane bank rather than separate islands
  + Re-shaped the Barrett q-multiplier (mod_red_mult) from a box into a long Z-axis cylinder so it is silhouette-distinct from the boxy shift/subtract-correct member next to it
  + Added a thin glass 'operand-return bus' (return_bus) bridging the reduction stage back over the array to the crossbar, making the conflict-free read-modify-write loop explicit in ISO/TOP
  + Updated facts[] and assembly_instructions to 8 modeled butterfly units and the cylindrical reduction silhouette
  · gap: Eight clusters at 1.1 m pitch make the central array dense; verify in the next render that the copper multipliers and add/sub blocks are not mutually occluded in ISO, and if so widen vertical separation or stagger multiplier heights
  · gap: The operand-return bus is a single straight bar; consider explicit arrowhead/cone geometry at the crossbar end so direction of return (reduction -> crossbar) is unambiguous rather than just a span
  · gap: The exploded cluster still only explodes one of eight BUs; a faint second ghost outline or a small '×8' label-block could communicate that all eight lanes are identical instances of the exploded datapath
